1385498046875, -0.0244140625, 0.005523681640625, 0.0261993408203125, -0.018218994140625, 0.0083770751953125, 0.0005297660827636719, 0.0008602142333984375, -0.01161956787109375, 0.008209228515625, 0.008941650390625, -0.00844573974609375, -0.0036411285400390625, -0.037567138671875, -0.00873565673828125, -0.0255126953125, -0.0006237030029296875, 0.018096923828125, -0.00841522216796875, -0.01910400390625, -0.019683837890625, 0.0012979507446289062, -0.01174163818359375, 0.0103607177734375, 0.00968170166015625, 0.0550537109375, -0.03277587890625, -0.020904541015625, -0.0003399848937988281, 0.0127716064453125, -0.023834228515625, -0.0022525787353515625, 0.0149383544921875, -0.00197601318359375, 0.00624847412109375, -0.042572021484375, -0.01119232177734375, -0.0179290771484375, -0.013458251953125, 0.039825439453125, -0.00592803955078125, 0.0367431640625, -0.047821044921875, -0.002529144287109375, -0.03546142578125, -0.00997161865234375, 0.00021660327911376953, -0.01128387451171875, -0.005046844482421875, -0.0037841796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "This brief work extends the permanental process model to incorporate fixed effects. By assigning a prior to the fixed effects coefficients, we demonstrate that, as the prior becomes increasingly non-informative, the intensity function of the permanental process can be derived using the representer theorem. This results in a natural decomposition into a fixed effects component and a function belonging to a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS). We further show that the limiting equivalent kernel defines an RKHS whose squared norm precisely corresponds to the limiting penalty. This framework offers a clear scientific interpretation of permanental process models and facilitates the integration of domain knowledge into the estimation procedure."}}